(   2. The volatile Qualifier:) Tj
T*
(   - Semantics: Tells the compiler that the variable's value may be changed at any time by) Tj
T*
(   entities outside the current program code \(e.g., hardware registers, interrupt service) Tj
T*
(   routines, concurrent threads\).) Tj
T*
(   - Optimization Suppression: Prevents compiler from caching the variable in a CPU register or) Tj
T*
(   eliminating redundant reads/writes.) Tj
T*
(   - Mandatory Use-Cases:) Tj
T*
(   a. Memory-Mapped I/O hardware registers:) Tj
T*
(   volatile uint32_t * const UART_STATUS_REG = \(volatile uint32_t *\)0x4000C000;) Tj
T*
(   b. Global flags modified inside Interrupt Service Routines \(ISRs\):) Tj
T*
(   volatile int timer_flag = 0;) Tj
T*
(   c. Variables shared across threads without explicit mutex barriers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Combining const and volatile:) Tj
T*
(   - Legal syntax: volatile const uint32_t *RO_REG = \(volatile const uint32_t *\)0x4000C004;) Tj
T*
(   - Meaning: The software program is forbidden from writing to it \(const\), but hardware may alter) Tj
T*
(   its value dynamically \(volatile\), such as a read-only hardware status register.) Tj
T*
() Tj
