(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the sizeof operator, compile-time evaluation, and differences between sizeof\('a'\) in C versus C++.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The sizeof Operator Mechanics:) Tj
T*
(   1. Operator Characteristics:) Tj
T*
(   - Compile-Time Operator: sizeof is an operator, NOT a function. It evaluates the byte footprint) Tj
T*
(   of types or expressions during the compilation phase without generating runtime instructions.) Tj
T*
(   - Return Type: Evaluates to size_t \(an unsigned integer type defined in <stddef.h>, printed) Tj
T*
(   with %zu\).) Tj
T*
(   - Expression Non-Evaluation: Expressions inside sizeof\(\) are never executed at runtime:) Tj
T*
(   int x = 5;) Tj
T*
(   size_t s = sizeof\(x++\); /* x is NOT incremented; x remains 5 */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Syntax Variations:) Tj
T*
(   - For data types: Parentheses mandatory: sizeof\(int\), sizeof\(double\).) Tj
T*
(   - For variables / expressions: Parentheses optional: sizeof x, sizeof\(x + 5.0\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Critical Difference: sizeof\('a'\) in C vs C++:) Tj
T*
(   - In ANSI C:) Tj
T*
(   Character constants like 'a', 'Z', '\\n' have type int!) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, sizeof\('a'\) == sizeof\(int\), which is 4 bytes on typical 32-bit/64-bit systems.) Tj
T*
(   - In C++:) Tj
T*
(   Character constants are strictly typed as char literals!) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, sizeof\('a'\) == sizeof\(char\) == 1 byte.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Size of Pointers vs Pointed Data:) Tj
T*
(   Regardless of what type a pointer points to \(char*, int*, double*, struct*\), the pointer) Tj
T*
(   variable itself stores a memory address:) Tj
T*
(   - 4 bytes on a 32-bit architecture.) Tj
T*
(   - 8 bytes on a 64-bit architecture.) Tj
T*
