(   - Division \(/\): Behavior depends strictly on operand types:) Tj
T*
(   a. Floating-Point Division: If either operand is float/double, fractional quotient is preserved) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., 7.0 / 2.0 = 3.5\).) Tj
T*
(   b. Integer Division: If both operands are integers, the quotient is truncated toward zero,) Tj
T*
(   discarding fractional parts \(e.g., 7 / 2 = 3\).) Tj
T*
(   - Modulo \(%\): Computes remainder of integer division. Operands MUST be integers. Applying % to) Tj
T*
(   float/double causes compile error.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. C99 Truncation Toward Zero Standard:) Tj
T*
(   Prior to C99, integer division rounding with negative operands was implementation-defined.) Tj
T*
(   C99 codified the algebraic invariant:) Tj
T*
(   \(a / b\) * b + \(a % b\) == a) Tj
T*
(   Integer division strictly truncates towards zero \(chops fractional digits\):) Tj
T*
(   - Positive division: 7 / 3 = 2, 7 % 3 = 1) Tj
T*
(   - Negative numerator: -7 / 3 = -2, -7 % 3 = -1 [Check: \(-2\)*3 + \(-1\) = -7]) Tj
T*
(   - Negative denominator: 7 / -3 = -2, 7 % -3 = 1 [Check: \(-2\)*\(-3\) + 1 = 7]) Tj
T*
(   - Both negative: -7 / -3 = 2, -7 % -3 = -1 [Check: 2*\(-3\) + \(-1\) = -7]) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Rule of Sign for Modulo:) Tj
T*
(   In ISO C99, the sign of the modulo remainder \(a % b\) matches the sign of the numerator \(a\),) Tj
T*
(   regardless of denominator b!) Tj
T*
(   - \(+a\) % \(+b\) -> Positive) Tj
T*
(   - \(-a\) % \(+b\) -> Negative) Tj
T*
(   - \(+a\) % \(-b\) -> Positive) Tj
T*
(   - \(-a\) % \(-b\) -> Negative) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Division by Zero Pitfall:) Tj
T*
(   Division by integer zero \(x / 0 or x % 0\) triggers undefined behavior, resulting in an) Tj
T*
(   immediate hardware fault \(SIGFPE signal on Linux, crash on Windows\). Programmers must guard) Tj
T*
(   denominators with validations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
