(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss Bitwise Operators \(&, |, ^, ~, <<, >>\) with binary bit-level walkthroughs and practical systems programming applications.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Bitwise Operators and Low-Level Manipulation in C:) Tj
T*
(   Bitwise operators operate directly on the individual bits of integer operands \(char, short,) Tj
T*
(   int, long\). They cannot be applied to float or double.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. The Six Bitwise Operators:) Tj
T*
(   - Bitwise AND \(&\): 1 if both corresponding bits are 1; otherwise 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Bitwise OR \(|\): 1 if either or both corresponding bits are 1; otherwise 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Bitwise XOR \(^\): 1 if corresponding bits differ; 0 if identical.) Tj
T*
(   - Bitwise NOT \(~\): Unary operator; inverts each bit \(0 to 1, 1 to 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Left Shift \(<<\): Shifts bits left, filling vacated low-order bits with 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Right Shift \(>>\): Shifts bits right. For unsigned types, vacated high-order bits are) Tj
T*
(   zero-filled \(logical shift\). For signed types, sign bit is preserved on most architectures) Tj
T*
(   \(arithmetic shift\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Binary Step-by-Step Walkthrough:) Tj
T*
(   Let A = 13 \(binary: 00001101\) and B = 10 \(binary: 00001010\):) Tj
T*
(   - A & B:) Tj
T*
(   00001101 \(13\)) Tj
T*
(   & 00001010 \(10\)) Tj
T*
(   ----------) Tj
T*
(   00001000 = 8 decimal) Tj
T*
(   - A | B:) Tj
T*
(   00001101 \(13\)) Tj
T*
(   | 00001010 \(10\)) Tj
T*
(   ----------) Tj
T*
(   00001111 = 15 decimal) Tj
T*
(   - A ^ B:) Tj
T*
(   00001101 \(13\)) Tj
T*
(   ^ 00001010 \(10\)) Tj
T*
(   ----------) Tj
T*
(   00000111 = 7 decimal) Tj
T*
(   - ~A \(8-bit signed\):) Tj
T*
(   ~ 00001101 = 11110010 = -14 decimal \(Two's complement\)) Tj
T*
