(   [Problem Statement]: Write a complete C program demonstrating Brian Kernighan's algorithm to count set bits in an integer with step-by-step analysis.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Brian Kernighan's Bit-Counting Algorithm:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Principle:) Tj
T*
(   Standard bit counting loops through all 32 bits one by one.) Tj
T*
(   Brian Kernighan's algorithm optimizes this by jumping directly between set bits \(1s\):) Tj
T*
(   The expression: n = n & \(n - 1\)) Tj
T*
(   clears the least significant set bit \(rightmost 1\) of integer n in each iteration!) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, the loop executes exactly K times, where K is the number of set bits \(O\(K\) time) Tj
T*
(   complexity\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Mathematical Trace for n = 44 \(Binary: 00101100\):) Tj
T*
(   - Iteration 1:) Tj
T*
(   n = 00101100 \(44\)) Tj
T*
(   n - 1 = 00101011 \(43\)) Tj
T*
(   n & \(n-1\) = 00101000 \(40\) -> Cleared bit 2. Counter = 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Iteration 2:) Tj
T*
(   n = 00101000 \(40\)) Tj
T*
(   n - 1 = 00100111 \(39\)) Tj
T*
(   n & \(n-1\) = 00100000 \(32\) -> Cleared bit 3. Counter = 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Iteration 3:) Tj
T*
(   n = 00100000 \(32\)) Tj
T*
(   n - 1 = 00011111 \(31\)) Tj
T*
(   n & \(n-1\) = 00000000 \(0\) -> Cleared bit 5. Counter = 3.) Tj
T*
(   - n is now 0; loop terminates. Total set bits = 3.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Complete ANSI C Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int count_set_bits\(unsigned int n\) {) Tj
T*
(   int count = 0;) Tj
T*
(   while \(n > 0\) {) Tj
T*
(   n &= \(n - 1\); /* Strips lowest set bit */) Tj
T*
(   count++;) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
(   return count;) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int main\(void\) {) Tj
T*
(   unsigned int test_values[] = {0, 7, 44, 255, 1024, 0xFFFFFFFF};) Tj
T*
