(   [Problem Statement]: Explain in-place variable swapping using bitwise XOR without a temporary variable, including mathematical proof and self-swapping hazards.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   In-Place Variable Swapping Using Bitwise XOR:) Tj
T*
(   1. The XOR In-Place Algorithm:) Tj
T*
(   Two integer variables a and b can be swapped without auxiliary memory using three XOR) Tj
T*
(   operations:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: a = a ^ b;) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: b = a ^ b;) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: a = a ^ b;) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Proof of Correctness:) Tj
T*
(   Let original values be A and B:) Tj
T*
(   - Initial State: a = A, b = B) Tj
T*
(   - After Step 1: a = A ^ B, b = B) Tj
T*
(   - After Step 2: b = a ^ b) Tj
T*
(   = \(A ^ B\) ^ B) Tj
T*
(   = A ^ \(B ^ B\) [Associative property]) Tj
T*
(   = A ^ 0 [Identity: x ^ x = 0]) Tj
T*
(   = A [Result: b now holds original A]) Tj
T*
(   - After Step 3: a = a ^ b) Tj
T*
(   = \(A ^ B\) ^ A [Substitute current a and b]) Tj
T*
(   = \(A ^ A\) ^ B) Tj
T*
(   = 0 ^ B) Tj
T*
(   = B [Result: a now holds original B]) Tj
T*
(   The variables are completely swapped!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Self-Swapping Hazard \(Identical Memory Address Trap\):) Tj
T*
(   - Fatal Bug: If a and b point to the SAME memory address \(e.g. &a == &b in an array partition) Tj
T*
(   loop\):) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: *a = *a ^ *b => *a = *a ^ *a => *a = 0!) Tj
T*
(   The memory location is permanently zeroed out! Subsequent steps XOR with 0 and cannot recover) Tj
T*
(   the data.) Tj
T*
(   - Solution: Always check address inequality prior to performing XOR swap:) Tj
T*
(   void safe_swap\(int *x, int *y\) {) Tj
T*
(   if \(x != y\) {) Tj
T*
(   *x ^= *y;) Tj
T*
(   *y ^= *x;) Tj
T*
(   *x ^= *y;) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
