(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the rules of Implicit Type Promotion \(Usual Arithmetic Conversions\) and explain potential precision loss pitfalls.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Implicit Type Promotion and Arithmetic Conversions:) Tj
T*
(   When an expression involves operands of differing types, C performs implicit type conversions) Tj
T*
(   following standardized rules:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Integer Promotion:) Tj
T*
(   In any arithmetic expression, small integer types \(signed char, unsigned char, short, unsigned) Tj
T*
(   short\) are automatically promoted to int \(or unsigned int if int cannot represent all values\)) Tj
T*
(   before calculation.) Tj
T*
(   Example:) Tj
T*
(   char c1 = 100, c2 = 50;) Tj
T*
(   char c3 = c1 + c2; /* c1 and c2 are promoted to int \(100 + 50 = 150\), then truncated to char */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Usual Arithmetic Conversions \(Hierarchy of Types\):) Tj
T*
(   When binary operators combine different types, the lower-ranked type is converted up to the) Tj
T*
(   higher-ranked type:) Tj
T*
(   Rank Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
(   long double > double > float > unsigned long long > long long > unsigned long > long > unsigned) Tj
T*
(   int > int) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Signed vs Unsigned Trap:) Tj
T*
(   If an int is compared with an unsigned int, the signed int is implicitly converted to unsigned) Tj
T*
(   int!) Tj
T*
(   Code Example:) Tj
T*
(   int a = -1;) Tj
T*
