(   [Problem Statement]: Explain integer overflow and underflow in C, contrasting signed undefined behavior with unsigned modular wraparound.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Integer Overflow and Underflow in C:) Tj
T*
(   1. Conceptual Definition:) Tj
T*
(   Occurs when an arithmetic operation attempts to create a numeric value outside the) Tj
T*
(   representable range of a given integer type.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Signed Integer Overflow \(Undefined Behavior\):) Tj
T*
(   - Signed types \(int, short, long\) use Two's Complement encoding.) Tj
T*
(   - ISO C Standard Explicit Rule: Signed integer overflow is strictly UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR!) Tj
T*
(   - Compilers assume signed overflow NEVER occurs. Consequently, code like:) Tj
T*
(   if \(x + 1 > x\) { ... }) Tj
T*
(   may have the check completely removed by optimizing compilers \(-O2/-O3\), opening critical) Tj
T*
(   security vulnerabilities!) Tj
T*
(   - On most hardware without optimization, INT_MAX + 1 wraps to INT_MIN \(-2,147,483,648\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Unsigned Integer Overflow \(Defined Modular Wraparound\):) Tj
T*
(   - ISO C Standard Rule: Unsigned integers NEVER overflow!) Tj
T*
(   - They follow strict modulo arithmetic: Computations wrap modulo 2^N \(where N is the number of) Tj
T*
(   bits in the type\).) Tj
T*
(   - For an unsigned 32-bit int \(range 0 to 4,294,967,295\):) Tj
T*
(   4294967295U + 1U == 0U \(completely well-defined behavior\).) Tj
T*
(   0U - 1U == 4294967295U \(modular underflow\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Safe Overflow Detection Code:) Tj
T*
(   To test if adding signed integers a and b will overflow without triggering undefined behavior:) Tj
T*
(   #include <limits.h>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int safe_add\(int a, int b, int *result\) {) Tj
T*
(   if \(\(b > 0 && a > INT_MAX - b\) || \(b < 0 && a < INT_MIN - b\)\) {) Tj
T*
(   return -1; /* Overflow error condition detected */) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
(   *result = a + b;) Tj
T*
(   return 0; /* Operation safe */) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
